<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · 1888 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sakura Sakura</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">さくら さくら</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Sakura, Sakura</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Japanese folk song about cherry blossoms in spring, often the first tune children learn on the koto.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>Edo period (melody); words in their present form Meiji period; first printed 1888</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; words in their present form from the Meiji period, a further verse published by the Ministry of Education in 1941)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Sakura, Sakura&quot; is a Japanese folk song about spring, the season of cherry blossoms, and it is often sung abroad as a song that stands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Its melody is not ancient, as is often believed: it was a popular urban tune of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The melody has been popular since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a>, and its words took their present form then. One theory holds that the song is a parody of &quot;Saita sakura&quot;, thought to have been a koto song of the Edo period; other theories about the original words exist.</p><p>The tune uses the in scale (the miyako-bushi pentatonic scale), in quadruple metre and in three parts (ABBAC) over 14 bars. Because its range is narrow, it suits instruments with few notes, such as the shakuhachi or the Native American flute. An arrangement by the Ongaku Torishirabe-gakari was printed in 1888 in a Collection of Japanese Koto Music for beginning koto students at the Tokyo Academy of Music. It is still often the first piece koto beginners learn, since every phrase can be played on neighbouring strings; the koto is tuned for it in the hira-joshi tuning.</p><p>In 1941 the Ministry of Education published a new verse in the school songbook Uta no hon, placed before the original verse, which became the second.</p><p>The cherry blossom carries deep meaning in Japanese culture as a sign of the shortness of life. In Buddhist reading the five petals stand for the five skandhas that make up a human being, which arise, are beautiful for a short time and fall; the blossom is a chief example of mono no aware, the beauty of passing things. Falling flowers have also served as a metaphor for warriors who died in battle and for the souls of the dead.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The song pictures spring, the season when the cherry trees bloom. Its mood is quiet and admiring, and behind it lies the meaning the blossom holds in Japanese culture: a beauty that lasts only a short time.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Japanese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Japanese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">櫻さくら。彌生の空は。見渡すかぎり。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Sakura, sakura, Yayoi no sora wa, miwatasu kagiri,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cherry blossoms, cherry blossoms! The sky of Yayoi, as far as the eye can see:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">霞か雲か。にほひぞいづる。いざや〳〵。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kasumi ka kumo ka, nioi zo izuru. Iza ya, iza ya,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is it mist, or is it cloud? A fragrance comes forth. Come now, come now,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">見にゆかむ。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">mi ni yukan.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us go and see.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional (Edo period); as printed in 1888. Text from <a href="https://ja.wikisource.org/wiki/%E3%81%95%E3%81%8F%E3%82%89%E3%81%95%E3%81%8F%E3%82%89" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (modified Hepburn).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: modified Hepburn of the words as they are read and sung (modern pronunciation); the historical kana spelling of the original is not transliterated letter by letter (にほひ nioi, いづる izuru, ゆかむ yukan; letter by letter nihohi, idzuru, yukamu). 櫻 and 彌生 are old character forms (桜, 弥生), read sakura, Yayoi. 〳〵 is the repeat sign for いざや, written out (iza ya, iza ya). The 。 phrase marks become commas, with a full stop where the sense ends.</li><li>The edition's line division is kept: three phrases on each of the first two lines, the last phrase alone.</li><li>line 1: Yayoi, the third month of the old lunar calendar (roughly April), kept as a name.</li><li>line 2: 'mist or cloud' are the masses of blossom seen from afar (implied, not said).</li><li>line 2: 'nioi' in classical Japanese is a fragrance but also a glowing colour or beauty; 'a fragrance comes forth' keeps the commoner reading, with the visual sense possible.</li><li>line 2: 'iza ya', 'come now', an urging call.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
